   "title": "Inside the Black Box of Class Size: Mechanisms, Behavioral Responses, and Social Background",
   "year": "2016",
   "venue": "Journal of Public Economics",
   "coauthors": [
    "Björn Öckert",
    "Hessel Oosterbeek"
   ],
   "contribution": "Studies the mechanisms (parental responses, teacher behavior, heterogeneity by background) behind class-size effects.",
   "confidence": "medium"
  },
  {
   "title": "Dynamic Treatment Assignment: The Consequences for Evaluations Using Observational Data",
   "year": "2008",
   "venue": "Journal of Business & Economic Statistics",
   "coauthors": [
    "Per Johansson"
   ],
   "contribution": "Shows how dynamic program assignment complicates evaluation with observational data and proposes an appropriate estimator.",
   "confidence": "medium"
  }
 ],
 "persona": {
  "summary": "A Scandinavian labor and education economist who combines equilibrium search theory of unemployment insurance with credible empirical evaluation of education and labor market policies. His empirical work draws on Swedish register data. He is a long-serving committee member and, I believe, a former chair.",
  "research_lens": "Policy-relevant labor economics. Key concerns are how institutions (UI, schooling rules, hiring practices) shape incentives, human capital and matching, and what their long-run welfare consequences are. His early work is theoretical (optimal UI in search models); his later work is mostly quasi-experimental.",
  "methods_and_evidence": "Regression discontinuity and other quasi-experimental designs; long-run follow-up with linked administrative registers; equilibrium search models; normative analysis of optimal insurance design. He pays attention to mechanisms and heterogeneity, not only average effects.",
  "what_they_value_in_contributions": "Inferred from his record: credible identification of causal effects; evidence on long-run outcomes; contributions that tie theory to measurable policy parameters; work that changed how policy is evaluated or designed. Presenting the 2021 prize for natural experiments and causal inference fits with this.",
  "likely_questions_in_deliberation": [
   "Is the causal identification convincing, and has the evidence been replicated across settings and countries?",
   "Do the findings hold up in the long run, and what are the mechanisms behind them?",
   "How does the empirical work connect to a theoretical framework that permits welfare and policy conclusions?",
   "Has the contribution changed how economists or policymakers design or evaluate labor market and education policy?",
   "Are general-equilibrium or displacement effects accounted for when moving from partial-equilibrium estimates to policy?",
   "Whose contribution was truly original versus incremental within the research program?"
  ],
  "committee_experience": "He has served on the committee for several years, probably since around the mid-to-late 2010s. To my recollection he chaired it around 2021–2022, including the 2021 announcement (Card; Angrist and Imbens). The exact start year and chair term are uncertain."
 },
 "knowledge_limits": "My knowledge comes from training data reaching roughly 2024–early 2025. I am unsure of the exact years of his committee service and chair term, his full list of recent papers and their final venues, and any administrative roles beyond his IFAU and Stockholm University association. Some coauthor lists and venues above are given with medium confidence. I have no information about his views on specific 2026 candidates."
}

